## ATV and quad bike are the same thing

Start here, because it wastes more booking time than anything else.

**ATV** stands for all-terrain vehicle. A quad is an ATV. Two listings using the two words are selling the same machine, and neither word tells you anything about what you are getting.

What does tell you something is the **riding area** and the **engine**, in that order. We split our six quads by area rather than by model for that reason: a fenced zone for children from six, a marked two-kilometre boundary square from twelve, and the open red dunes from fourteen.

A 250cc quad in a boundary square and a 250cc quad on a dune face are not the same experience. The number on the listing is the part that looks important and the part that matters least.

## What the first hour actually contains

Roughly, and it is more structured than most people expect.

1. **Arrival and paperwork.** Photo ID, the booking checked, payment settled at the base if you have not paid.
2. **Helmet fitting.** Not optional and not negotiable, and worth getting properly tight rather than politely tight.
3. **Controls briefing.** Throttle, brake, kill switch, and the line the group will ride.
4. **Warm-up on flat sand.** Five minutes or so, away from the dunes.
5. **The ride.** A lead guide sets the pace, a sweep rider stays at the back so the group never splits.
6. **Photo stop and return.**

The whole thing runs from our base at Al Awir on the Dubai-Hatta road, about 45 minutes from Downtown Dubai. Hotel pickup inside Dubai is free.

## The warm-up is a test, and nobody says so

This is the part worth knowing in advance, because knowing it makes it easier rather than harder.

While you are riding around on flat ground getting used to the throttle, the guide is deciding whether you are on the right machine. If somebody is clearly struggling, they get moved down: a smaller engine, or out of the open desert and into the boundary area.

That happens regularly and it is not a judgement. A quad responds to weight transfer in a way nothing else on the road does, and some people take twenty minutes to find it rather than five. The alternative, which is sending a rider who cannot steer onto a dune face because they paid for it, is how people get hurt.

If you would rather not have that conversation in front of your friends, book the boundary area to start with. Moving up on the day is easy and nobody has ever minded. Moving down is the awkward direction.

## Which area a first-timer should book

**The boundary area**, almost always. It is a marked two-kilometre square on gentle sand, from age twelve, on a 150 to 350cc machine with a guide present, and it starts at AED 89.

The open desert from fourteen is the real red dunes, and it is worth doing on a second visit or if you have ridden before. The [Yamaha Raptor 700cc](/quad-bike-dubai/) is a sport quad and it is not a beginner machine in any useful sense of the word.

The [full price ladder](/quad-bike-dubai/price/) starts at AED 75 for thirty minutes in the fenced kids area. Every quad biking Dubai price is per quad, and the double-seat machines carry a passenger free, so two people ride for one price.

## Book the hour

Thirty minutes is a genuine taster and it is the right call if the base is on your way somewhere and you just want the feel of it.

For most first-time riders it ends about ten minutes after they stop being nervous, which is the point at which it starts being fun. An hour is the sweet spot and it is the booking we recommend to people travelling in from [Sharjah, Ajman or Abu Dhabi](/locations/), where a thirty-minute session does not justify the drive.

## What actually goes wrong

Three things, in order of how often.

**Booking the biggest engine because it sounds better.** The riding area decides the day and the engine decides the last ten percent of it.

**Underestimating how physical it is.** An hour on a quad in soft sand uses your arms, shoulders and legs. In August it uses more of them. If anyone in the group would prefer to be seated, with a wheel and a harness, a [dune buggy](/dune-buggy-dubai/) is the same desert with none of that.

**Not saying it is a first time.** We put first-timers in the right place when we know, and the booking message is where to say it.

We have run this base since 2020 and taken more than 17,000 guests into the Lahbab dunes. The ones who come back are almost never the ones who booked by engine size.
